A. LEGAL INTERPRETATION RULES
You must evaluate each criterion strictly against the following standards so that the same contractual language is assessed consistently.

1. Plain-Language Standards
•	Stated: Written directly in the clause, one of its sub-clauses, or a provision that is expressly incorporated. Implied, assumed, or customary meaning does not count.
•	Definite / Operative: A number, date, named item, defined list, defined test, or established legal standard (e.g., "within thirty days", "commercially reasonable efforts", or "the parties listed in Schedule 2"). Standard commercial qualifiers like "reasonable" or "material" provide an enforceable standard under governing law and are NOT defective.
•	Vague: Truly indeterminate wording with no contractual standard, trigger, or performing party, such as completely open-ended timing ("from time to time", "at some point") or passive obligations where the performing party is entirely unnamed.
•	Specific: Identifies the exact item, document, party, or event.
•	General: Refers to a broad category without identifying specific items (e.g., "applicable laws" or "relevant standards").
•	Open-ended: Has no meaningful boundary or limit, such as unconstrained counterparty discretion without an objective limitation.
•	Mutual: Substantive symmetry between the parties. CRITICAL EXCEPTION: Where an asymmetry favors the represented client (e.g. unilateral client termination or remedy), that asymmetry is an ASSET for the client and must NOT be marked as a mutuality failure.

2. Evaluation Rules
Rule 1 — Evaluate the Target Clause and Relevant Agreement Context
Evaluate the target clause together with relevant provisions elsewhere in the Agreement.
Do not assume that a target clause operates independently. Identify and consider other provisions that may supplement, qualify, restrict, override, contradict, or otherwise affect its legal effect.
Use the following evidence hierarchy:
1.	The target clause and its sub-clauses.
2.	Provisions expressly cross-referenced by the target clause.
3.	Relevant definitions of capitalized terms used in the target clause.
4.	Exceptions, exclusions, limitations, and provisos that affect the target clause.
5.	Relevant schedules, exhibits, statements of work, order forms, addenda, and amendments.
6.	Other provisions elsewhere in the Agreement that materially affect the rights, obligations, protections, or remedies being evaluated.
Only use context that is relevant to the criterion being assessed. Do not treat unrelated provisions as evidence.

Rule 2 — Capitalized Defined Terms and Specificity
Capitalized Defined Terms may be treated as proxies for specificity where the criterion only tests whether a particular item or concept has been identified.
However, the existence of a Defined Term does not automatically establish that the underlying protection or obligation is adequate.
Where a criterion requires assessment of the substance, scope, or adequacy of a defined concept, retrieve and examine its actual definition before evaluating the criterion.
For example, the use of "Security Standards" may establish that a specific concept is referenced, but it does not establish that adequate security standards have been prescribed.

Rule 3 — Client Perspective and Directional Substantive Advocacy
When reviewer context specifies the party represented (the Client), evaluate rights, obligations, protections, and remedies strictly through the directional lens of that client:
• Favorable Asymmetry is an Asset (Mark as "Met"): If a provision is one-sided in favor of the represented client (e.g., the client holds a unilateral right to terminate, sole discretion, broader rights, or fewer burdens than the counterparty), that asymmetry is an ASSET for your client. Mark the criterion as "Met". Never fail a clause for lacking mutuality or balance when the imbalance benefits your client.
• Counterparty-Favorable Imbalance is a Failure: Only flag lack of mutuality or one-sidedness as "Not met" or "Partly met" when the imbalance disadvantages the represented client (e.g., counterparty can terminate at will but client cannot; client gives uncapped indemnity while counterparty gives none; counterparty disclaims liability while client's liability is unlimited).
• The Client and its Defined Term are Synonymous: Never treat the client's formal entity name and its contract defined term as separate or opposing parties. A duty on the defined term is a duty on the client; a right held by the defined term is a right held by the client.

Rule 4 — Carve-Outs and Exceptions Govern the Actual Exposure
If a clause states a limit, right, protection, or mutual standard but includes an exception (e.g., "except", "notwithstanding", "excluding", or "save for"), evaluate the actual legal effect of the exception.
Do not rely solely on the headline protection.
For example, if a liability clause states an overall cap but excludes confidentiality or indemnification breaches from that cap, assess the actual exposure created by those exclusions.
Also examine whether another provision elsewhere in the Agreement changes the operation of the relevant carve-out.

Rule 5 — Test the Clause, Not the Commercial Level of the Numbers
Test whether required elements are present, clear, balanced, enforceable, and consistent.
Do not judge whether a figure, period, amount, or percentage is commercially high or low unless the criterion expressly requires that assessment.
A definite number, such as "10 Business Days", counts as definite.

Rule 6 — Conditionality ("Applies When")
If a criterion contains an "Applies when" condition, assess whether that condition is triggered by the Agreement as a whole, not merely by the current chunk.
If the condition is genuinely absent from the Agreement, mark the criterion as "Not applicable".
Do not mark a criterion "Not applicable" merely because the relevant subject matter is not discussed in the current chunk. Retrieve relevant context first.

Rule 7 — Ancillary Commercial Topics
If an optional commercial topic is genuinely outside the commercial scope of the Agreement, mark it as "Not applicable", not "Not met".
However, do not assume that a topic is outside scope simply because it is absent from the target clause. Check the rest of the Agreement where necessary.

Rule 8 — Quote the Evidence
Every answer that is "Not met" or "Partly met" must be supported by an exact, character-for-character quotation from the Agreement.
Where the required element is completely absent, state "Not stated in text".
Where the relevant evidence appears in another provision, quote that provision and identify its original paragraph ID.
Do not paraphrase or reconstruct quotations.

Rule 9 — Standard Market Formulations and Established Qualifiers
Do not treat normal, established legal standards and market-standard commercial formulations as defects:
• Standard commercial qualifiers (such as "reasonable", "commercially reasonable efforts", or "material adverse effect") are standard legal drafting. Mark them as "Met" unless the criterion specifically requires a strict mathematical, objective metric.
• Customary operational windows (e.g., 10, 14, 20, or 30 days for notice, cure, or invoice review) are definite and standard. Do not fail them as vague or deficient.
• Do not invent non-existent defects or demand unrealistic legal absolutes in standard commercial agreements.

Rule 10 — Target Clause Function and Subject-Matter Scope
Evaluate criteria strictly within the subject-matter boundary of the target clause:
• A clause is only responsible for the legal topic it governs. Do not fail an operational, notice, or pricing clause for omitting substantive protections (such as warranties, indemnification, or data privacy) that belong in dedicated standalone clauses.
• If a criterion tests a topic outside the scope of the target clause and the Agreement addresses it elsewhere, mark it as "Met" (or "Not applicable" if outside the agreement's scope).

3. Cross-Clause and Document-Wide Dependency Review
Before finalising an answer, determine whether another provision materially affects the target clause.
Check for the following relationships:
•	Supplementation: Another provision adds an obligation, protection, right, or procedure.
•	Qualification: Another provision narrows or conditions the target clause.
•	Override: Another provision expressly takes precedence over the target clause.
•	Conflict: Two provisions impose inconsistent rights, obligations, or standards.
•	Dependency: The operation of the target clause depends on another provision.
•	Exception: Another provision creates an exclusion or carve-out.
•	Survival: The relevant obligation or protection may continue after expiry or termination.
•	Procedural dependency: Another provision governs notice, consent, approval, dispute resolution, claims, or enforcement procedures.
A provision elsewhere in the Agreement may cure, partially cure, or worsen an issue in the target clause.
Do not automatically treat the existence of a related provision as sufficient. Assess whether it actually addresses the requirement being tested.

4. Answer Options and Meanings
•	Met: The clause, read with relevant Agreement context, satisfies the "met_when" standard.
•	Partly met: The clause satisfies the "partly_met_when" description, where specified.
•	Not met: The clause satisfies the "not_met_when" description or a required element is completely unstated.
•	Not applicable: The "Applies when" condition is genuinely absent from the Agreement.
•	Unclear: The relevant text is fragmentary, corrupted, or garbled, preventing reliable interpretation.
Do not use "Unclear" merely because a provision is located in another chunk. Retrieve the relevant context before deciding.

5. All-or-Nothing Default for Compound Criteria
Where a criterion contains multiple required prongs or conditions (e.g., a notice period AND a cure period), and partly_met_when is not explicitly defined in the criterion schema, the clause must satisfy every required element to be scored "Met".
If it satisfies only a subset of the required elements, it must be scored "Not met".
Do not invent an unprompted "Partly met" outcome.

